{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/515969-getting-a-game-started","id":515969,"title":"Getting a game started","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-05-16T02:00:54-07:00","id":515969,"title":"Getting a game started","content":"Hey,<br /> <br /> since Wraith is the only gameline in wich I reallly posses almost no experience but really want to play it eventually I'd love some sugesstions how to find into the game when the whole troupe is new to it.<br /> <br /> First of all. I ran a single game of wraith and I kinda thought it was a good start for the game, but since we never played a second session doubts are becoming stronger over time. It took place e in the wild west and I used the game Fiasco in terms of character creation (I think a good game of Fiasco makes a great preludium for the characters in a wod-setting). The players started as humans and only knew we were going to play a world of darkness setting. They took a lot of effort to find goals (&quot;needs&quot; how they are called in fiasco), describe their relation towards each other and find out wich people, places or objects matter to them most.<br /> We started playing and they began to execute the first steps of their plannings before they all died in a mysterious way, leaving lots of plot unresolved, needs unfullfilled to become passions and their dearest friends and stuff becoming their fetters (the players had completely free hand in choosing these passions and fetters, but I sugested them what qualified if they didn't come up with ideas.)<br /> <br /> The session went pretty well, everyone seemed to have fun (even shadow play was introduced and they did a quite good job at it as far as I can tell) but at least one player told me afterwards he was disappointed when the characters died; because all the plotlines that were going on when they lived were so interesting and he'd rather liked to play on as a mortal or vampire to continue in the real world. Well, I got the feeling he's not the only player who thinks that way.<br /> <br /> On one hand this some kinda felt perfectly right for wraith, since the player really feels similar to the character, being betrayed for his life and having so much unresolved bussines to attend that it could fill more than one chronicle.<br /> On the other hand the disappointment the players felt might have been to strong so that it might've bcome frustrating for them, wich obviously isn't good at all.<br /> <br /> Thats my only experience I ever had with wraith. I'd really like to play or run a game again, but I'd die (pun intendet) for some suggestions how I can start an introductory game to wraith everyone can enjoy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1235-eadee","name":"Eadee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-16T09:29:22-07:00","id":516239,"title":"","content":"That you have a troupe willing to give it a try - props to that! Personally, I think the player that is a bit butt hurt simply wants to do one of the other WoD games.<br /> <br /> Anywho, nonsense aside I would say start small. I'm new to Wraith, too, and after reading it I would imagine one would ideally gradually build and add the concepts rather than simply dump them on the characters from the get go.<br /> <br /> Think of it like any series. Some people are cool with jumping into a TV series with lots of weird references. That's good for them. For some, however, it's better if things start at a street-level, so to say, then build up.<br /> <br /> Those are just some of my thoughts though. Glad somebody could manage to find players willing to give it a try.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5979-rabidpigmy","name":"rabidpigmy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5979_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-16T09:32:13-07:00","id":516241,"title":"","content":"It seems like they got invested in the characters they were playing and not in the game you wanted to play. It sounds cool to create people with full real actualized lives to use as background for future wraiths, after all, that is a big part of what Wraith is all about. But, if you make up a character with a full real actualized life and all of a sudden the ST takes it away except in the most basic sketched out way, and your interaction with everything that was important to you is going to be tortuously difficult and tinged/tainted with heaps and heaps of sorrow and regret.... It does seem like its going to be hit or miss. Some players may LOVE it, but a lot of players are going to feel like they were cheated of a lot what they put into the character they just made. <br /> <br /> I only once ran a Wraith game where the players spent any time as living sorts and everyone know it was going to be a Wraith game, so they were waiting for the shoe to drop... They weren't at all surprised when the plane they were all on went down. That was the first session. Session 2 was getting Reaped and so on. <br /> <br /> There is an inherent problem with this sort of game across White Wolf. Absent some definitive rules about playing &quot;newbies&quot; the rules assume the character has had enough time to get familiar with their new &quot;skin&quot; as it were. A newly deceased wraith straight from the Cauls is probably going to be clueless about their Passions (until they happen across some mortals doing X) or about their Fetters (until something bad happens to them or they meet a &quot;friendly&quot; Monitor or get enough Arcanoi &quot;oomph&quot; to handle that on their own or they happen to go &quot;home&quot; and suddenly feel that Y is VERY VERY VERY important to them). They are also unlikely to develop the full slate of Arcanoi that a starting wraith gets per character creation rules. Similar issues with several of the Backgrounds as well. Many are not intrinsic. They take some cultivation that the newly dead just won't have. Some of the games have dealt with this to one degree or another, but Wraith, not so much.<br /> <br /> The other times, I ran the game as written. The players were all wraiths that had been dead for awhile and we either built them as a circle or we had an external source (a &quot;Mr. Jones&quot; type) collect them together. It worked out much better in the latter setting when they were all Hierarchy, since they were obeying orders and that gave them a reason to stay together until all the characters were invested enough in the plot to actually WANT to hang out with these former strangers. <br /> <br /> If you want to continue the game you've started (which doesn't seem like an impossible thing... all the right pieces needed within the context of the game are already there), you might jut want to have a sit-down and see if that interests your group. You never know, they might have &quot;gotten over the shock&quot; of the deaths and want to continue based on the postmortum fun it sounds like you all had, they might be convince-able, or they might be really really into it as it happened. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-16T10:50:16-07:00","id":516315,"title":"","content":"Also, it depends on what the level of experience your troupe you're introducing Wraith has in general - WoD, etc. If they've never played before, maybe pre-gen characters tailored toward an introductory story. Their fetters and passions can be designed into the story that way and the players can be introduced to these concepts. Maybe arcanoi can be slowly introduced based on reactions to danger or seeking their loved ones that they wish to get back in touch with. The Shadow can play a role, of course, if they're the same troupe and they're comfortable with that mess. It's a matter of feeling out and roleplaying those elements.<br /> <br /> Keep it simple and build upward adding things on like modules. You throw some some kind of problem that the PCs have to tangle with. Maybe if you want to introduce harrowings, you can have an NPC who survived the crash wrestling with a personal shadow get shocked into a harrowing. Maybe they discover their loved ones died in the accident and that immediately drives them over the edge. Perhaps they are pursued by unfriendly redneck spectres with dueling banjos. They flee into the nearest town and find themselves in an altogether weird world. The Reaper who helped them get out of the caul may be some lowbie who simply serves to hide them and warn them against some of the dangers. Something like that.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't over think it and add in the complex societal elements of wraith until a much more later. I would have to reread the books, but it seemed relatively easy to setup fresh wraiths. It's simply a matter of how you build the PCs and what's made available to them (if the players have their hands in generating them). If anything, build them up as skin bags and then introduce wraithly elements over them. Exceptions are perhaps Awareness, and some other elements that may just need translating from mortal to wraith. (Alertness becomes Awareness?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5979-rabidpigmy","name":"rabidpigmy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5979_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-05-24T18:54:52-07:00","id":527235,"title":"","content":"It depends if you want to play through a Prelude or not, or just keep it to general character background. I know that 1st edition mentions a line that your wraith character has died within the past 30 years, so with the Freebie points and all the stuff added onto them, as said above it's perhaps better to kick the players off as their Wraiths and maybe have flashback sessions where they were mortal? It could be interesting to play Wraith in modern day and see their characters as mortals in the 1980s. It kind of adds another element when the life your characters have left behind has moved on to something different. What of loved ones that act as their Fetters and any Passions they have linked to them... They're going to be up to 30 years older now. Your ex-spouse may be settled and have a grown-up child with someone else now... The young slip you fell for before passing will be getting grey hairs now.<br /> Maybe it's just me, but introducing that element into a Wraith campaign gives it a bit more narrative oomph without the bother of having to do a first session as mortals or what have you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3951-wander","name":"wander","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"409"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}